<p><a href="/documentation">Documentation</a> / Browse courses</p>
<h1>Browse courses</h1>
<p class="lead">
  Explore the demonstration semester catalog and choose the course
  whose study groups you want to see.
</p>

<h2>Before you begin</h2>
<p>
  <a href="/quickstart">Launch StudyHub</a>.
  You can browse the course catalog even when no groups have been created.
</p>

<h2>Step-by-step instructions</h2>
<ol>
  <li>On the main window, click <strong>Show all</strong>
      to clear any previous course selection and search.</li>
  <li>Open the dropdown next to <strong>Course</strong>.</li>
  <li>Browse the available demonstration courses.</li>
  <li>Select a course, such as <strong>CS101</strong>.
      The group list updates to show groups for that course.</li>
  <li>To explore another course, open the dropdown and select it.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Show all</strong> to return to groups across all courses
      and clear the search.</li>
</ol>

<figure>
  <img src="/images/studyhub-courses.png"
       alt="StudyHub with CS101 selected in the Course dropdown and its study group displayed">
  <figcaption>
    Selecting CS101 filters the list to groups associated with that course.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>What to expect</h2>
<p>
  A course can exist in the catalog without having any study groups.
  If the list is empty, you can
  <a href="/features/create-group">create a group</a> for that course.
</p>

<aside class="notice">
  <strong>Demonstration data:</strong>
  This catalog is an example semester catalog, not an official university
  course listing or a connection to a registration system.
</aside>

<p>
  Next: <a href="/features/browse-groups">Browse groups for a course</a>
</p>

